{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"pwd && sed -n '1,240p' AGENTS.md && printf '\\\\n--- docs/AGENTS.md ---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,260p' docs/AGENTS.md && printf '\\\\n--- RULES.md ---\\\\n' && if [ -f RULES.md ]; then sed -n '1,260p' RULES.md; fi\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"pwd && sed -n '1,240p' AGENTS.md && printf '\\\\n--- docs/AGENTS.md ---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,260p' docs/AGENTS.md && printf '\\\\n--- RULES.md ---\\\\n' && if [ -f RULES.md ]; then sed -n '1,260p' RULES.md; fi\"","aggregated_output":"/Users/marekkucharski/projects/.pm-worktrees/vgt/issue-8\n# AGENTS.md\n\nThis repository is managed autonomously by [proj-mgr](https://github.com/mrkkucharski/proj-mgr).\nAn orchestrator periodically checks this repo's GitHub issues and resumes/starts an\nagent (you) to work through them, without a human in the loop on every step.\n\n## How work is organized\n\n- **GitHub Issues are tasks.** Each open issue carries labels that tell the orchestrator\n  and every agent its current state. Labels must be kept accurate at all times.\n\n### Status labels\n\nEvery open issue must have exactly one `status:*` label at all times. These are set\nby the orchestrator (when starting, reviewing, or fixing work) and by you (when\nfinishing, blocking, or handing off). Keep them in sync with reality:\n\n| Label | Meaning | Who sets it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `status:queued` | Not started yet, waiting to be picked up | Orchestrator / humans |\n| `status:working` | An agent is actively working on it right now | Orchestrator (on start) |\n| `status:ready-for-review` | Implementation complete, waiting for a reviewer | Implementer agent |\n| `status:needs-fix` | Reviewer rejected it; implementer must fix | Reviewer agent |\n| `status:blocked` | Needs a human decision or external resource | Implementer/fix agent |\n\n**IMPORTANT -- label cleanup rules you must follow:**\n\n1. **When you finish and mark ready-for-review:** remove `status:working`.\n   ```\n   gh issue edit <N> --repo <repo> --add-label status:ready-for-review --remove-label status:working\n   ```\n\n2. **When you get blocked:** remove the current work label too, not just add blocked.\n   ```\n   gh issue edit <N> --repo <repo> --add-label status:blocked --remove-label status:working\n   ```\n   (Use `--remove-label status:needs-fix` instead if you were in a fix cycle and\n   the orchestrator had already replaced `status:needs-fix` with `status:working`\n   before you started -- the label on the issue when you receive it is `status:working`.)\n\n3. **Do NOT set `status:blocked` because the issue has sub-tasks.** The orchestrator\n   automatically holds a parent issue until all its sub-issues are closed; you do not\n   need to (and must not) label it blocked for that reason. Using `status:blocked` for\n   sub-task ordering will prevent the issue from being started even after sub-tasks\n   finish. Only use `status:blocked` when you genuinely need a human decision or an\n   external resource you cannot obtain yourself.\n\n4. **Reviewer agents -- when you accept and close an issue:** also remove `status:working`.\n   The orchestrator sets `status:working` when it starts a review session. Closing the\n   issue without removing the label leaves it visually stale.\n   ```\n   gh issue close <N> --repo <repo>\n   gh issue edit <N> --repo <repo> --remove-label status:working\n   ```\n\n5. **Reviewer agents -- when you reject:** remove `status:working`, not `status:ready-for-review`\n   (the orchestrator already removed that when it started your review session).\n   ```\n   gh issue edit <N> --repo <repo> --add-label status:needs-fix --remove-label status:working\n   ```\n\n### Priority labels\n\nEvery issue should carry a `priority:*` label. The orchestrator uses it to decide\nwhich issue to start next. **When you create sub-issues or follow-up issues, always\ninclude a priority label:**\n\n```\ngh issue create --repo <repo> --title \"...\" --body \"...\" \\\n  --label status:queued --label priority:medium\n```\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `priority:high` | Blocker; start as soon as possible |\n| `priority:medium` | Important but not an emergency |\n| `priority:neutral` | Routine work (default when absent) |\n| `priority:low` | Nice-to-have, defer when higher-priority work exists |\n| `priority:optional` | Stretch goal; only if nothing else is queued |\n\nNumeric labels (`priority:1` through `priority:5`) are also accepted, where 5 = high\nand 1 = optional.\n\n### Agent labels\n\n`implementer:claude` and `implementer:codex` are set by the orchestrator to record\nwhich agent last implemented or fixed an issue. `reviewer:claude` and\n`reviewer:codex` record which agent last reviewed it. Do not set or remove these\nyourself.\n\n### Fix-count labels\n\n`fix-count:N` is set by the orchestrator to track how many review/fix cycles an issue\nhas gone through. Do not set or remove these yourself.\n\n## Sub-issues express ordering\n\nIf an issue has sub-issues, the orchestrator will not start it until every one of its\nsub-issues is closed. Break a large task into sub-issues when parts must be done first;\nthe sub-issues get picked up on their own, and the parent becomes startable once they're\nall closed. **Never label a parent blocked just because its sub-issues are still open.**\n\n## docs/ holds project docs\n\n`docs/GOAL.md` defines what \"done\" means for the whole project -- read it before\ndeciding the project is finished or needs more work.\n\n## .pm/ is orchestrator bookkeeping\n\n`.pm/state.json` and `.pm/log.ndjson` are owned by the orchestrator. Don't hand-edit\nthese; the orchestrator owns them.\n\n## RULES.md\n\n`RULES.md` (if present) is the shared rule set inherited from proj-mgr: scope\ndiscipline, git hygiene, no silent scope changes, no secrets, log everything, stay bounded.\n\n## Ground rules\n\n- Work only on the issue you were assigned. Read its title, body, and prior comments first.\n- Commit as you go with clear messages; push when you reach a stable point.\n- Keep labels accurate throughout your work (see Status labels section above).\n- Never force-push, never rewrite other issues' history, never touch secrets/`.env` files.\n\n## Project-specific rules\n\nRead docs/AGENTS.md for the project context and any project-specific rules and\nconventions. It is the place to record how *this particular* project works --\nits architecture, file layout, naming conventions, domain rules, and any\nper-command guidance -- as opposed to the orchestration rules above, which are\nthe same for every proj-mgr project.\n\n--- docs/AGENTS.md ---\n# Project-specific agent instructions\n\nThis file provides project-specific guidance to the coding agent (you) when\nworking in this repository, in addition to the AGENTS.md located at the repo\nroot. The root AGENTS.md covers how proj-mgr orchestration works and is the same\nfor every managed project; this file is where *this* project's own context and\nrules live.\n\n## Project overview\n\n**vgt** (virtual guitar teacher) is a CLI that prepares REAPER projects for\nguitar practice. This project is **phase 0**: locate/open a REAPER project,\nstore vgt's own state next to it, create a `[vgt]`-managed area, and add one\ntrack that mirrors the original song — all non-destructively and idempotently.\nSee `docs/GOAL.md` for the exact scope and `docs/phase1-song-prep-plan.md` for\nwhere this is heading (out of scope now).\n\n## Environment assumptions\n\n- **REAPER is installed on the machine.** You may assume a working REAPER\n  install (target: 7.x; the test fixture was saved with 7.65) at the standard\n  macOS location `/Applications/REAPER.app`, with the CLI binary at\n  `/Applications/REAPER.app/Contents/MacOS/REAPER`. It is fine to invoke REAPER\n  (e.g. to run a ReaScript action) or rely on its presence in tests; you do not\n  need to bundle, download, or install it.\n- Platform is macOS (Apple Silicon). Python 3.11 + `uv` per the plan's stack.\n\n## Test project\n\nA real REAPER project fixture lives at **`test/Reaper Project/`** — use it for\ndeveloping and testing project locating, reading, and non-destructive\naugmentation against a genuine `.RPP` (not a synthetic one).\n\n- `Reaper Project.RPP` — REAPER 7.74 project, 3 tracks: `Click`, and two song\n  tracks `The Seven Rivers (Full March - 3_00)` and `Paris Metro Punk`.\n- `Media/*.mp3` — the two song audio files, referenced by the `.RPP` via\n  **relative** paths (`FILE \"Media/...\"`), so they resolve regardless of\n  checkout location. `Click` is a REAPER-native click source with no file.\n- REAPER-generated peak caches (`Media/peaks/`, `*.reapeaks`) and auto-backups\n  (`Backups/`) are **git-ignored** — REAPER regenerates peaks on open, so don't\n  rely on them being present and don't commit them.\n\nTreat this as a **read-mostly fixture**: exercise vgt against a copy, and never\ncommit vgt's mutations back into `test/`. The tracks here are the user's own —\nper the non-destructive rule, vgt must only ever touch `[vgt]`-prefixed objects\nit creates.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Everything vgt creates in a REAPER project carries a `[vgt]` prefix (tracks,\n  regions, folders); only `[vgt]`-owned objects may be modified or removed.\n- Commit style: conventional commits (see git history).\n\n## Domain rules\n\n- **Non-destructive & idempotent** are hard invariants — never overwrite,\n  rename, or delete tracks/objects vgt didn't create; re-running a command must\n  not duplicate or corrupt anything.\n- Prefer manipulating the project through the REAPER API (ReaScript action) over\n  editing `.RPP` text, so changes appear live in the user's open project and\n  REAPER handles construction correctly.\n\n--- RULES.md ---\n# Global rules for autonomously-managed projects\n\nThese rules apply to every project managed by proj-mgr, in addition to whatever is in\nthat project's own `AGENTS.md`. They are copied into each new project repo as\n`RULES.md` at creation time.\n\n1. **Scope discipline.** Only touch what the current issue requires. Don't refactor,\n   reorganize, or \"clean up\" unrelated code while working an issue.\n2. **Git hygiene.** Never force-push. Never amend or rewrite commits other than your\n   own most recent uncommitted work. Commit in small, reviewable chunks.\n3. **No silent scope changes.** If the issue as written seems wrong, too big, or\n   ambiguous, say so in an issue comment and mark `status:blocked` rather than\n   guessing.\n4. **Secrets.** Never read, print, log, or commit secrets/credentials/`.env` files. If\n   a task appears to require credentials you don't have, mark `status:blocked`.\n5. **Logging.** Every meaningful state change (issue created/updated, artifact\n   produced, decision made) should be visible in the issue thread and/or\n   `.pm/log.ndjson` history -- not just implicit in code changes. Future ticks (and\n   the human owner) rely on this trail.\n6. **Done means verified + published.** Before considering an issue finished, run the\n   relevant tests/checks, confirm they pass, commit the work, and push it. Only then\n   close the issue. If tests fail or publish/close steps fail, don't treat the issue\n   as done.\n7. **Stay bounded.** Don't try to do the whole project in one sitting. Do a coherent\n   chunk of work, leave the repo in a working state, and stop -- you'll be resumed\n   automatically.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/GOAL.md; git status --short --branch; git log --oneline --decorate -12; gh issue view 8 --repo mrkkucharski/vgt --comments; gh pr view 13 --repo mrkkucharski/vgt --comments --json number,title,state,baseRefName,headRefName,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,body,commits,files\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/GOAL.md; git status --short --branch; git log --oneline --decorate -12; gh issue view 8 --repo mrkkucharski/vgt --comments; gh pr view 13 --repo mrkkucharski/vgt --comments --json number,title,state,baseRefName,headRefName,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,body,commits,files\"","aggregated_output":"author:\tmrkkucharski\nassociation:\towner\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nChecked the declared dependency before implementation: #7 (analysis foundation + schema v2) remains open with `status:needs-fix`, and this branch does not contain its sidecar/stage interface. I have not duplicated or cherry-picked its unreviewed implementation into #8; this issue can proceed once #7 is accepted/merged or the orchestrator provides its accepted foundation.\n--\nauthor:\tmrkkucharski\nassociation:\towner\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nImplemented the tempo/beat/downbeat stage on top of #7's analysis foundation.\n\nNote on dependency ordering: #7 (\"Sub 1\") is still open (`status:needs-fix`, not a native GitHub sub-issue of #8), so this branch merges in `issue-7-phase-1-analysis-foundation-vgt-sidecar` directly to get the `.vgt` schema v2 / stage-cache framework this issue builds on. Once #7 lands on `main`, this branch should rebase cleanly (no conflicts expected — #7's commits are already included verbatim via the merge).\n\n## What's implemented\n- `src/vgt/tempo.py`: `detect_beats` (madmom DBN beat+downbeat primary, librosa `beat_track` fallback) and `build_tempo_grid` (least-squares constant-BPM fit vs. piecewise-linear spans, decided by the global fit's relative residual — see the tempo-map rule in `docs/GOAL.md`). Also renders the click-over-mix verification artifact.\n- `madmom` is isolated behind an optional `madmom` extra (`pyproject.toml`), confirmed fragile exactly as the issue warned: madmom 0.16.1 (its last release) doesn't even import on Python 3.11 (`collections.MutableSequence` was removed in 3.10, `pkg_resources` needs `setuptools<81`). The librosa fallback is therefore the practical default path and ships unconditionally.\n- Wired into `vgt analyze`'s `tempo` stage; output includes `bpm`, `downbeat_offset_seconds`, `time_signature`, `mode` (`constant`/`piecewise`), `residual_seconds`, `spans` (when piecewise), `backend`, and `click_artifact_path`.\n\n## Verified against the fixture\n- `vgt analyze` on `test/Reaper Project` detects a clean constant 120.0 BPM grid via the librosa fallback in ~2s and writes `Reaper Project.vgt-tempo-click.wav` next to the sidecar (gitignored — it's a regenerated verification artifact, not source).\n- Manual tempo correction (`human_verified: true`) persists across re-runs (existing generic stage-cache test, now exercised against the real detector).\n- `uv run pytest`: 17 passed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [x] Beat grid (bpm, downbeat offset, time signature, mode, residual) written to the sidecar for the fixture.\n- [x] Click-over-mix artifact generated (audibly locks — verified by inspecting duration/channels/sample rate; I can't audition it myself, but the render pipeline is straightforward `librosa.clicks` mixed over the loaded source).\n- [x] A tempo/downbeat correction persists across re-runs.\n--\nauthor:\tmrkkucharski\nassociation:\towner\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nAddressed both review points:\n\n1. **Rebase onto main.** #7 is now merged (`21968dc`), so this branch is rebased cleanly onto current `main` (single commit cherry-picked, no merge of the stale unreviewed #7 branch). #7's accepted fixes — the analysis-stage cache test signature and the quoted-JSON ReaScript write — are untouched, since they already live on `main`. Fixed a stale test in `tests/test_analysis.py` that still assumed the old 2-arg detector signature (`detect_tempo` takes `project_path` too, for the click artifact path).\n\n2. **madmom fallback on runtime failure.** `_madmom_beats` now catches processing failures (not just `ImportError`) and falls back to librosa — madmom can import fine and still blow up once its models run against real audio on modern Python/NumPy. Added `test_detect_beats_falls_back_to_librosa_when_madmom_processing_fails`, which stubs an importable-but-broken madmom module to cover this path.\n\n`uv run pytest`: 20 passed. `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD`: clean.\n--\nauthor:\tmrkkucharski\nassociation:\towner\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nFix cycle 2: I couldn't find written reviewer feedback anywhere (issue comments, PR #13 reviews/review-comments, and the issue/PR timelines are all empty aside from my own prior progress notes), so I did an independent pass over `src/vgt/tempo.py` looking for correctness bugs a reviewer would plausibly flag.\n\nFound and fixed a real bug in `_piecewise_spans` (the piecewise-linear tempo-map splitter):\n\n- The greedy split loop's `for i in range(segment_start + _MIN_SPAN_BEATS, n + 1)` bound was computed once before the loop started. After a split rewound `segment_start` backward, the loop kept advancing `i` from where it left off instead of re-checking `_MIN_SPAN_BEATS` from the new `segment_start` -- on tempo that changes rapidly, this degenerated into near single-beat spans (confirmed: 200 oscillating-tempo beats produced 198 spans instead of respecting the intended 4-beat minimum).\n- Also fixed a related off-by-one: `close_span(i - 1)` drops the beat that triggered the CV overflow, so a span closed exactly when the window first reaches `_MIN_SPAN_BEATS` actually only had `_MIN_SPAN_BEATS - 1` beats in it.\n\nReplaced the `for` loop with a `while` loop that recomputes the bound after every split, and starts each window one beat past `_MIN_SPAN_BEATS` so the *closed* span (not the pre-split window) always respects the minimum. Added `test_piecewise_spans_never_split_below_the_minimum_span_size` covering this.\n\nThis only affects the piecewise-tempo-map path (rapid/irregular tempo changes); the fixture's constant-BPM grid was and remains unaffected (`120.004` BPM, `constant` mode, residual `0.01647s`).\n\n`uv run pytest`: 21 passed.\n--\nauthor:\tmrkkucharski\nassociation:\towner\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\nFix cycle 3: as in cycle 2, no written reviewer feedback was findable (issue comments, PR #13 reviews/review-comments all empty aside from my own prior notes). Checked `.pm/log.ndjson` directly for the review verdict this cycle produced no entry either, so I treated this as another independent-verification pass.\n\nFound one concrete, fixable problem:\n\n- **Branch had drifted behind `main` again** (`.pm/` bookkeeping commits only — no code conflicts) and PR #13 was showing `mergeable: CONFLICTING`. Rebased cleanly onto current `main`; PR #13 now reports `mergeStateStatus: CLEAN` / `mergeable: MERGEABLE`.\n\nBeyond that, I re-verified the actual detector output rather than just re-reading the code:\n\n- Ran `vgt analyze` against the real fixture (`test/Reaper Project`): constant `120.004` BPM, residual `0.01647s`, librosa backend (madmom still doesn't run on this Python/NumPy, as documented).\n- Cross-checked the detected beat grid against independent `librosa.onset.onset_detect` onsets on the same audio: median offset **5.8ms**, mean **6.7ms** — the grid does lock to the actual audio, not just to itself.\n- Manually set `human_verified: true` with an overridden BPM on the sidecar and re-ran `analyze`: the correction persisted unchanged, confirming acceptance criterion 3 against the real fixture (not just the existing generic stage-cache unit test).\n- Re-verified `_piecewise_spans` (fixed in cycle 2) with a synthetic 80→160→80 BPM sequence: spans tile the full timeline with no gaps/overlaps beyond the intended shared boundary beat.\n\n`uv run pytest`: 21 passed. `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD`: clean.\n--\n{\"baseRefName\":\"main\",\"body\":\"Closes #8\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\nSee issue #8 for details.\",\"commits\":[{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-07-18T12:13:07Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"marekkucharski@mini.local\",\"id\":\"\",\"login\":\"\",\"name\":\"Marek Kucharski\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Sonnet 5\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-07-18T18:42:53Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Implements the tempo analysis stage on top of #7's stage-cache framework:\\nmadmom's DBN beat+downbeat trackers are the primary backend (isolated behind\\nan optional `madmom` extra since its last release predates modern\\nPython/NumPy and needs Cython + a legacy ABI + the deprecated pkg_resources\\nAPI), falling back to librosa's beat_track (bundled by default) when madmom\\nisn't installed or fails to import.\\n\\nBeat intervals are least-squares fit to either a single constant BPM or a\\npiecewise-linear tempo map, based on the global fit's relative residual\\n(docs/GOAL.md's tempo-map rule); mode, residual, and backend are recorded\\nalongside bpm/downbeat_offset/time_signature in the sidecar. Each run also\\nrenders a click-over-mix WAV next to the sidecar so the grid can be checked\\nby ear.\\n\\nVerified against the real fixture: detects a clean 120 BPM constant grid\\nin ~2s via the librosa fallback (madmom itself can't even import on this\\nPython version, confirming the fragility the issue calls out).\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"feat: detect tempo & beat/downbeat grid from the reference mix\",\"oid\":\"d9325fd21d92f7275696c9caa6a26f7bbc67c5c7\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-07-18T18:22:39Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"marekkucharski@mini.local\",\"id\":\"\",\"login\":\"\",\"name\":\"Marek Kucharski\"},{\"email\":\"noreply@anthropic.com\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjgxODQ3\",\"login\":\"claude\",\"name\":\"Claude Sonnet 5\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-07-18T18:42:53Z\",\"messageBody\":\"Rebases the tempo stage onto main now that #7's schema-v2 foundation is\\nmerged, dropping the stale merge of the unreviewed issue-7 branch (its\\naccepted fixes -- the analysis-stage cache test signature and the\\nquoted-JSON ReaScript write -- already live on main untouched).\\n\\nmadmom's fallback to librosa previously triggered only on ImportError.\\nSince madmom's last release predates modern Python/NumPy, it can also\\nimport successfully and then blow up once its models run against real\\naudio -- that failure mode must fall back too, not propagate.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: rebase onto main and fall back to librosa on madmom runtime failure\",\"oid\":\"e3b037129c9ac951285f91045f54d2075423af1e\"},{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-07-18T18:31:09Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"marekkucharski@mini.local\",\"id\":\"\",\"login\":\"\",\"name\":\"Marek Kucharski\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-07-18T18:42:53Z\",\"messageBody\":\"The greedy splitter's for-loop range was computed once before the loop\\nstarted, so after a split rewound segment_start, subsequent windows no\\nlonger re-checked _MIN_SPAN_BEATS -- rapid tempo changes could produce\\nnear single-beat spans. Also fixes an off-by-one: closing a span drops\\nthe beat that triggered the CV overflow, so the window must reach one\\nbeat past _MIN_SPAN_BEATS before a split is eligible, or the closed\\nspan ends up one beat short of the minimum.\",\"messageHeadline\":\"fix: enforce minimum span length in piecewise tempo-map splitting\",\"oid\":\"a30da36b879e7c3d39651eff6f2cfcd81a67f243\"}],\"files\":[{\"path\":\".gitignore\",\"additions\":4,\"deletions\":0,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"},{\"path\":\"README.md\",\"additions\":20,\"deletions\":0,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"},{\"path\":\"pyproject.toml\",\"additions\":19,\"deletions\":1,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"},{\"path\":\"src/vgt/analysis.py\",\"additions\":26,\"deletions\":13,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"},{\"path\":\"src/vgt/tempo.py\",\"additions\":206,\"deletions\":0,\"changeType\":\"ADDED\"},{\"path\":\"tests/test_analysis.py\",\"additions\":9,\"deletions\":1,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"},{\"path\":\"tests/test_tempo.py\",\"additions\":127,\"deletions\":0,\"changeType\":\"ADDED\"},{\"path\":\"uv.lock\",\"additions\":833,\"deletions\":0,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"}],\"headRefName\":\"issue-8-phase-1-tempo-beat-downbeat-grid\",\"mergeStateStatus\":\"UNKNOWN\",\"number\":13,\"reviewDecision\":\"\",\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Phase 1: tempo & beat/downbeat grid\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
